05. BRAND EXPERIENCE AND HUMAN PSYCHOLOGY
==============================================================

Design around how clients discover, evaluate, and purchase services.

Do not manipulate visitors or create false urgency.

Use five principles throughout the website.

1. RECOGNITION

Help visitors recognize a need they already have.

Use clear phrases such as:
"My brand needs a better identity."
"I need a website."
"Customers cannot find my business."
"I need someone to manage my social media."
"I want to automate repetitive work."

Do not require visitors to understand technical terminology first.

2. PROGRESSIVE DISCLOSURE

Show the department first.

Reveal its services when expanded.

Reveal service details only after the user selects a service.

Do not expose every piece of information simultaneously.

3. CLEAR VISUAL HIERARCHY

Give every major section a clear purpose.

Avoid multiple competing headlines and too many buttons.

Use one prominent action and, where appropriate, one secondary action.

4. REDUCED FRICTION

Let visitors browse services and prices without registering.

Do not require a long questionnaire before seeing an offer.

Keep project qualification questions for a scheduled meeting
or subsequent discussion.

5. TRUST THROUGH TRANSPARENCY

Explain deliverables, exclusions, prices, discounts, payment status,
and the next action clearly.

Do not invent social proof.

Do not hide recurring costs.

Do not create fake countdowns or fabricated scarcity.

